This test file has a great harness for booting a CLI. But for the queue-lane re-sync test I need `providersQueueRuntime` with a probe-backed failover. That's closer to the `settings_flow_providers_queue_test.dart` harness.

Plan for the CLI wiring test — new file `test/cli/agent_cli_queue_model_resync_test.dart`:

```dart
// gh-1617 review r2 (IMPORTANT): a mid-run providers-queue failover must
// re-sync `agent.state.model` the way the roles lane's `_onRolesNotice`
// does — the watchdog's arm-time window reads `state.model`, so a stale
// boot-time entry keeps the OLD entry's streamIdleTimeoutMs patience
// while the new entry streams.
library;

import 'package:flutter_agent_harness/flutter_agent_harness.dart';
import 'package:test/test.dart';

import 'agent_cli_test_support.dart';

Model _model(String id, {String baseUrl = 'https://q.test/v1'}) => Model(...);
```

Wait — how does `ProviderQueueRuntime.build` build models? `buildProviderQueueChain` builds models from entries via the catalog (`buildCatalogModel(spec.name, entry.model, baseUrl: ...)`) — the queue lane doesn't use my `Model` objects; the entries carry providerType/model/baseUrl. The probe-backed streamFactory: `(kind, apiKey) => probe.streamForKey(apiKey)`.

For the CLI test I need to know the entry's resolved model ids — `queueRuntime.streamFunction.currentModel.id` gives them ('queue-a', 'queue-b').

Test sketch:

```dart
test('a mid-run queue failover re-syncs agent.state.model (watchdog window follows the streaming entry)', () async {
  final env = MemoryExecutionEnv(cwd: '/work');
  final io = FakeCliIO();
  // probe: key-head (queue-a) dies once with a 502; key-two (queue-b) serves.
  ...
  final runtime = ProviderQueueRuntime.build(
    ProviderQueueResolution(
      scope: ProviderQueueScope.env,
      entries: [entry-a, entry-b],
      notices: const [],
    ),
    secrets: {'K_A': 'a', 'K_B': 'b'},
    policy: const ModelRolesRetryPolicy(retriesPerEntry: 0),
    streamFactory: (kind, apiKey) => probe.streamForKey(apiKey),
  );
  final cli = AgentCli(
    config: AgentCliConfig(model: testModel, apiKey: '[REDACTED:Sensitive Value]', env: env, sessionRoot: '/sessions', providersQueueRuntime: runtime),
    io: io,
    streamFunction: FakeStreamFunction([textTurn('ok')]).call,
  );
  expect(cli.agent.state.model.id, 'queue-a'); // boot sync
  final run = cli.run();
  io.sendLine('hi');
  await waitForIt(() => !cli.isBusy, ...);
  io.sendLine('/exit');
  await run;
  expect(cli.agent.state.model.id, 'queue-b'); // resynced after failover — RED before fix
});
```

Wait — does the CLI's boot path with `providersQueueRuntime` REPLACE the stream function? Yes: `_agent.streamFunction = queueRuntime.streamFunction.call`. The `streamFunction:` param passed to AgentCli is the fallback when no queue. Fine.

